  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered strong to severe thunderstorms \npossible this afternoon into tonight.\n\nScattered thunderstorms are expected to continue to develop this\nafternoon ahead of a cold front across CPA and within a sfc\npressure trough in the southeast. SPC has continued the level \n2/5 (SLGT) severe t-storm risk which extends from the eastern \nslopes of the Allegheny Mtns eastward.\n\nMLCAPE of 1500 J/kg and 0-6 km shear near 30 kts in the north\nsuggests a few low end supercells may develop. Weaker shear and\nstronger CAPE in the SE suggests more pulse cells to start, but\nstorm motion from SW to NE is well aligned with the sfc pressure\ntrough so upscale growth into one or more slow moving / training\nlines is likely as we head into this evening. The strongest \ncells and clusters will be capable of producing scattered \ndamaging winds and at least isolated hail. Modestly curved low- \nlevel hodographs may also support some brief tornado potential.\nThe severe threat should persist into the evening before storms\nweaken/exit the eastern part of the CWA. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Flooding downpours possible this afternoon into \ntonight across portions of the Lower Susquehanna Valley \n\nA healthy influx of moisture via southwest flow will increase\nPWATs to 1.50-1.75\" across the Lower Susquehanna Valley by this\nafternoon. CAMs suggest the heaviest 2-4+ inch rain amounts \nwill remain east of the CWA, but still suggest local bullseyes \nof 1-2\" in our southeastern zones along the weak pre \nfrontal/lee sfc trough. A couple rounds of heavy t-storm \ndownpours are possible with rain rates 1-2\"/hr that could result\nin scattered areas of flash flooding. Based on the +RA signal \nin the hires ensembles and coordination with WFO PHI, a flash \nflood watch continues for Schuylkill, Lebanon, and Lancaster \nCounties.\n\nA drying trend is forecast Friday into the last weekend of \nAugust, thanks to high pressure building into the area behind \nthe cold front. This will likely setup a couple of foggy \nmornings (Friday and Saturday). Rainfall looks fairly limited \nheading into the first week of September; however despite the \nstart of meteorological Fall the summer is not over with temps \nexpected to rise above the historical average into the 80-90 \ndegree range.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Variable sky conditions for the lunar eclipse \ntonight.\n\nForecast viewing conditions for the lunar eclipse range from \nfair to poor tonight across central PA. The eclipse will begin \nat 10:34 pm EDT and last for about 3 hours, ending at 1:52 am \nEDT Friday. The worst view is most likely over the eastern half \nof CPA due to exiting storms and associated cloud cover. Clouds \nand low fog are also poised to develop in the NW during the \nlatter half of the eclipse. The eclipse will take place during \nAugust's full moon, commonly referred to as the Sturgeon Moon. \nIt will also be the last full moon of the summer with the next \nfull moon set to rise a few nights after the autumnal (Fall) \nequinox on September 22nd.",
